really leads me into what I really want

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:54.440
to talk about with you, which is kind of

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:56.480
the type of customer that you work with.

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:58.240
Your main customers are older folks or

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:00.440
people with disabilities. So, let's talk

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:02.080
a little bit about how you landed on

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:04.800
your specific type of customer.

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:06.840
>> So, I'm a product manager. I get on user

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:09.040
calls. Like, if I could just be on user

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:11.440
calls 24/7, that's all I would do. And

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:13.960
with Voicy, what I would realize is that

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:15.720
like 60 to 70% of the people were like

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:17.920
professionals like you and me. We were

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:20.080
already fast typers, and we wanted Voicy

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:22.680
to make us like 10% more productive. And

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:25.280
then like 20-30% of the time, I would

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:27.720
speak to someone who had a physical

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:30.800
disability or someone who was just a

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:32.480
senior person who wasn't as tech-savvy

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:34.680
and couldn't use their computer as much.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:36.320
Those guys loved the product. They

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:38.560
didn't care about any improvements. They

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:40.839
were like, "This MVP, I could live with

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:43.480
